{:edb/db-before-t 1912 :edb/db-after-t 1913 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535225 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:56:08.376Z" 13194139535225 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338535 13194139535225 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338542 13194139535225 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338556 13194139535225 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338549 13194139535225 true] [17592186134294 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535225 true] [17592186134294 1100 "Consider the vector field $F = xyzi + e^{-y}\\cos πxj + (e^{-y}\\sin πz + 2xz)k$.\n\nEvaluate $∇ \\cdot F$ at the point $(1, 0, 0)$." 13194139535225 false] [17592186134294 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,j+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" 13194139535225 true] [17592186134294 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338535 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338536 13194139535225 true] [17592186338535 1089 "q-105573" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338535 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535225 true] [17592186338535 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/3088d088689bbd0dfd33abe8240c6f02cceb257937fc1fccb8ba3df151dbfaab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338535 1113 #uuid "cb71e2f3-58c2-5fe5-b7fd-919a06f927d6" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338535 1145 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338537 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338538 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338539 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338540 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338541 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1041 "selection" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338541 13194139535225 true] [17592186338536 1110 #uuid "d260d722-a004-5ffb-8989-3c47ec75d87d" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338537 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338537 1111 #uuid "6694ff0b-ce44-5267-940f-03bc7eaa739d" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338538 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338538 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338538 1111 #uuid "04614be6-49ef-5da8-8cb7-8d07bfe2629f" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338539 1034 "I only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338539 1111 #uuid "5eecea38-b79a-5b54-9dd2-2616c15784b2" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338540 1034 "III only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338540 1111 #uuid "d5cd3210-ff40-58f5-a265-8951fd4a2f9e" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338541 1034 "II only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338541 1111 #uuid "f472db3d-2365-5ac8-aabd-622d96e4a11a" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338542 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338543 13194139535225 true] [17592186338542 1089 "q-105576" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338542 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535225 true] [17592186338542 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/cf932f4f02a216d5111da0748cb04f815a7d2e85af5db2f9282f5aefc359b816.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338542 1113 #uuid "1a9b7bd5-4597-5a75-b03e-038a1e45c570" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338542 1145 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338544 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338545 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338546 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338547 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338548 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1041 "selection" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338544 13194139535225 true] [17592186338543 1110 #uuid "664359e7-76a5-5834-98f5-947262b90783" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338544 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338544 1111 #uuid "e7589e4d-9501-5cb7-a8be-6a05142e9723" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338545 1034 "II and IV only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338545 1111 #uuid "93b3d037-d808-54ef-a3a5-b7d76867a9b7" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338546 1034 "I only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338546 1111 #uuid "ef1f6cee-c0e4-5688-91d5-b3fd28fbb35b" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338547 1034 "IV only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338547 1111 #uuid "2e511b20-f647-5935-b693-8f0e6e7ff1f8" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338548 1034 "II only" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535225 true] [17592186338548 1111 #uuid "a173872e-2039-5f10-be0a-23d2a52c4a34" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338549 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338550 13194139535225 true] [17592186338549 1089 "q-123200" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338549 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535225 true] [17592186338549 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338549 1113 #uuid "aa5cdcb7-c1f3-500e-aa56-d1ffbcb0b240" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338549 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338551 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338552 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338553 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338554 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338555 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1041 "selection" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338553 13194139535225 true] [17592186338550 1110 #uuid "43fd2ca8-4514-54e8-917a-8e0bba17222f" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338551 1034 "76" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338551 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535225 true] [17592186338551 1111 #uuid "2ec0cfae-efb5-578f-9a25-ccb00c9e91fe" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338552 1034 "84" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535225 true] [17592186338552 1111 #uuid "30613d7b-1211-51e8-a015-8b7f4b5597e0" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338553 1034 "96" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535225 true] [17592186338553 1111 #uuid "63efa5e9-7250-5e71-9cbd-b4667d2d7ce6" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338554 1034 "48" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535225 true] [17592186338554 1111 #uuid "a076a420-51a8-57e9-bea4-675a98b5ff2f" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338555 1034 "144" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535225 true] [17592186338555 1111 #uuid "6e5073d6-9aac-5387-ac87-532b3b27db45" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338556 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338557 13194139535225 true] [17592186338556 1089 "q-131193" 13194139535225 true] [17592186338556 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535225 true] [17592186338556 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/2434964